# 🔐 PortSwigger — Access Control Labs

This directory documents my practical study of Access Control vulnerabilities through PortSwigger Web Security Academy.

**Learning Platform:** [PortSwigger Web Security Academy](https://portswigger.net/web-security/access-control)

**Category:** Access Control

**Objective:** Understand, identify, exploit and prevent Broken Access Control vulnerabilities through hands-on security labs.

---

## 🎯 Learning Objectives

- Understand how access control works in web applications.
- Identify vertical, horizontal and context-dependent access controls.
- Practise privilege escalation.
- Identify unprotected functionality and authorization bypasses.
- Understand parameter-based access control.
- Discover and exploit IDOR vulnerabilities.
- Test authorization in multi-step processes.
- Understand Referer-based and location-based access controls.
- Learn secure authorization implementation and prevention.

---

## 📚 Learning Roadmap

The following sequence follows the Access Control learning path on PortSwigger Web Security Academy.

### 01. What Is Access Control?

Understand the different ways applications restrict access to resources and functionality.

- [ ] Vertical access controls
- [ ] Horizontal access controls
- [ ] Context-dependent access controls

### 02. Vertical Privilege Escalation

Understand how users gain access to functionality or permissions belonging to higher-privileged users.

- [ ] Unprotected functionality
- [ ] Parameter-based access control
- [ ] Platform misconfiguration
- [ ] URL-matching discrepancies

### 03. Horizontal Privilege Escalation

Understand how one user accesses another user's resources or functionality without permission.

- [ ] Study the concept
- [ ] Complete relevant practical labs

### 04. Horizontal to Vertical Privilege Escalation

Understand how horizontal access to another user's account can lead to higher privileges.

- [ ] Study the attack chain
- [ ] Complete relevant practical labs

### 05. Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR)

Understand how applications expose direct references to objects without properly checking authorization.

- [ ] Direct references to database objects
- [ ] Direct references to static files

### 06. Vulnerabilities in Multi-Step Processes

Understand how authorization weaknesses in individual workflow steps can allow unauthorized actions.

- [ ] Study multi-step authorization
- [ ] Complete relevant practical labs

### 07. Vulnerabilities in Referer-Based Controls

Understand why relying on the HTTP Referer header for authorization is insecure.

- [ ] Study Referer-based access control
- [ ] Complete relevant practical labs

### 08. Vulnerabilities in Location-Based Controls

Understand how location-related restrictions can be bypassed when applications trust client-controlled information.

- [ ] Study location-based access control
- [ ] Complete relevant practical labs

### 09. Preventing Access Control Vulnerabilities

Study secure access-control implementation and remediation.

- [ ] Enforce authorization server-side
- [ ] Apply least privilege
- [ ] Deny access by default
- [ ] Verify resource ownership
- [ ] Protect administrative functionality
- [ ] Validate authorization on every relevant request
- [ ] Implement centralized authorization checks

